Okay, let's evaluate the 'Mercedes-Benz CLS 500'.

The "CLS 500" designation, typically associated with a V8 petrol engine, was used in the first (C219) and second (C218) generations of the CLS. It represented a high-performance, luxurious option below the top-tier AMG models.

However, the *most recent* generation of the CLS (C257, 2018-present) does **not** offer a "CLS 500" model. The engine lineup for the C257 tops out with the CLS 450 (inline-six petrol) in the standard range and the AMG CLS 53 (high-performance inline-six petrol) as the sole AMG variant. There is no V8 option in the current CLS generation.

Since you asked for the *most recent version* related to the CLS 500 nameplate, the closest equivalent in terms of performance positioning within the current lineup (i.e., the highest performance model available) is the **Mercedes-AMG CLS 53**. While it uses an inline-six instead of the traditional V8, it occupies the performance pinnacle for the C257 generation, similar to how the CLS 500 sat near the top in previous generations.

Therefore, I will evaluate the **Mercedes-AMG CLS 53 (C257)** as the most relevant modern counterpart.

